#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:1
msgid "To `panic!` or Not to `panic!`"
msgstr "是否应该使用`panic!`还是`panic!`"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:3
msgid ""
"So, how do you decide when you should call `panic!` and when you should "
"return `Result`? When code panics, there’s no way to recover. You could call "
"`panic!` for any error situation, whether there’s a possible way to recover "
"or not, but then you’re making the decision that a situation is "
"unrecoverable on behalf of the calling code. When you choose to return a "
"`Result` value, you give the calling code options. The calling code could "
"choose to attempt to recover in a way that’s appropriate for its situation, "
"or it could decide that an `Err` value in this case is unrecoverable, so it "
"can call `panic!` and turn your recoverable error into an unrecoverable one. "
"Therefore, returning `Result` is a good default choice when you’re defining "
"a function that might fail."
msgstr ""
"那么，如何决定何时调用 `panic!`，何时返回 `Result` 呢？当代码出现故障时，是无"
"法恢复的。你可以为任何错误情况调用 `panic!`，无论是否有办法恢复，但这样你就代"
"表调用代码来决定某种情况是无法恢复的。当你选择返回 `Result` 的值时，你为调用"
"代码提供了选择的机会。调用代码可以选择以适合其情况的方式尝试恢复，或者决定在"
"这种情况下 `Err` 的值是无法恢复的，从而可以调用 `panic!`，将你的可恢复错误转"
"变为不可恢复的错误。因此，在定义可能失败的函数时，返回 `Result` 是一个很好的"
"默认选择。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:14
msgid ""
"In situations such as examples, prototype code, and tests, it’s more "
"appropriate to write code that panics instead of returning a `Result`. Let’s "
"explore why, then discuss situations in which the compiler can’t tell that "
"failure is impossible, but you as a human can. The chapter will conclude "
"with some general guidelines on how to decide whether to panic in library "
"code."
msgstr ""
"在示例、原型代码和测试等场景中，编写会导致程序崩溃的代码更为合适，而不是返回 "
"`Result`。接下来，我们将探讨为什么如此做，并讨论那些编译器无法判断出失败是不"
"可能的，但作为人类可以感知的情况。本章最后将提供一些关于如何在库代码中决定是"
"否使用崩溃处理的通用指导原则。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:20
msgid "Examples, Prototype Code, and Tests"
msgstr "示例、 原型代码和测试"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:22
msgid ""
"When you’re writing an example to illustrate some concept, also including "
"robust error-handling code can make the example less clear. In examples, "
"it’s understood that a call to a method like `unwrap` that could panic is "
"meant as a placeholder for the way you’d want your application to handle "
"errors, which can differ based on what the rest of your code is doing."
msgstr ""
"在编写示例来阐释某个概念时，如果包含了鲁棒性的错误处理代码，那么示例的清晰度"
"就会降低。在示例中，调用像 `unwrap` 这样的方法会被视为一个占位符，用来表示应"
"用程序处理错误的方式。实际上，错误处理的方式可能会因代码中其他部分的操作而有"
"所不同。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:28
msgid ""
"Similarly, the `unwrap` and `expect` methods are very handy when you’re "
"prototyping and you’re not yet ready to decide how to handle errors. They "
"leave clear markers in your code for when you’re ready to make your program "
"more robust."
msgstr ""
"同样，当您在进行原型设计时，还不确定如何处理错误时，`unwrap`和`expect`方法非"
"常实用。这些方法会在代码中留下明确的标记，以便您将来能够让程序更加健壮。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:33
msgid ""
"If a method call fails in a test, you’d want the whole test to fail, even if "
"that method isn’t the functionality under test. Because `panic!` is how a "
"test is marked as a failure, calling `unwrap` or `expect` is exactly what "
"should happen."
msgstr ""
"如果某个方法在测试中失败，你希望整个测试都失败，即使该方法并不是要测试的功"
"能。因为`panic!`是用来标记测试失败的，所以调用`unwrap`或`expect`正是应该发生"
"的情况。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:40
msgid ""
"<a id=\"cases-in-which-you-have-more-information-than-the-compiler\"></a>"
msgstr ""
"<a id=\"cases-in-which-you-have-more-information-than-the-compiler\"></a>"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:42
msgid "When You Have More Information Than the Compiler"
msgstr "当您拥有的信息比编译器所知道的更多时"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:44
msgid ""
"It would also be appropriate to call `expect` when you have some other logic "
"that ensures that the `Result` will have an `Ok` value, but the logic isn’t "
"something the compiler understands. You’ll still have a `Result` value that "
"you need to handle: Whatever operation you’re calling still has the "
"possibility of failing in general, even though it’s logically impossible in "
"your particular situation. If you can ensure by manually inspecting the code "
"that you’ll never have an `Err` variant, it’s perfectly acceptable to call "
"`expect` and document the reason you think you’ll never have an `Err` "
"variant in the argument text. Here’s an example:"
msgstr ""
"在存在其他逻辑来确保 `Result` 会有一个 `Ok` 值的情况下，调用 `expect` 也是合"
"适的。不过，这种逻辑并不是编译器能够理解的。你仍然需要处理 `Result` 这个值："
"无论你调用的是什么操作，通常都有失败的可能性，尽管在你的特定情况下这种失败在"
"逻辑上是不可能的。如果你通过手动检查代码来确保永远不会出现 `Err` 这种变体，那"
"么调用 `expect` 并在文档中说明你认为永远不会出现 `Err` 这种变体的原因是完全可"
"以接受的。以下是一个例子："

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:58
msgid "\"127.0.0.1\""
msgstr "\"127.0.0.1\""

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:60
msgid "\"Hardcoded IP address should be valid\""
msgstr "\"硬编码的 IP 地址应该是有效的”"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:64
msgid ""
"We’re creating an `IpAddr` instance by parsing a hardcoded string. We can "
"see that `127.0.0.1` is a valid IP address, so it’s acceptable to use "
"`expect` here. However, having a hardcoded, valid string doesn’t change the "
"return type of the `parse` method: We still get a `Result` value, and the "
"compiler will still make us handle the `Result` as if the `Err` variant is a "
"possibility because the compiler isn’t smart enough to see that this string "
"is always a valid IP address. If the IP address string came from a user "
"rather than being hardcoded into the program and therefore _did_ have a "
"possibility of failure, we’d definitely want to handle the `Result` in a "
"more robust way instead. Mentioning the assumption that this IP address is "
"hardcoded will prompt us to change `expect` to better error-handling code "
"if, in the future, we need to get the IP address from some other source "
"instead."
msgstr ""
"我们通过解析一个硬编码的字符串来创建一个 `IpAddr` 实例。我们可以看到 "
"`127.0.0.1` 是一个有效的 IP 地址，因此在这里使用 `expect` 是可行的。然而，拥"
"有一个硬编码且有效的字符串并不会改变 `parse` 方法的返回类型：我们仍然会得到一"
"个 `Result` 值，而且编译器会要求我们像处理 `Err` 这种可能性一样来处理 "
"`Result`，因为编译器没有足够的智能来识别这个字符串始终是一个有效的 IP 地址。"
"如果 IP 地址字符串是由用户提供的，而不是硬编码到程序中，那么肯定会有失败的可"
"能性，在这种情况下，我们当然希望以更稳健的方式来处理 `Result`。提及这个 IP 地"
"址是硬编码的这一假设，会促使我们在将来需要从其他来源获取 IP 地址时，将 "
"`expect` 更改为更好的错误处理代码。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:77
msgid "Guidelines for Error Handling"
msgstr "错误处理指南"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:79
msgid ""
"It’s advisable to have your code panic when it’s possible that your code "
"could end up in a bad state. In this context, a _bad state_ is when some "
"assumption, guarantee, contract, or invariant has been broken, such as when "
"invalid values, contradictory values, or missing values are passed to your "
"code—plus one or more of the following:"
msgstr ""
"建议在代码可能陷入不良状态时触发 panic 机制。在这种情况下，“不良状态”指的是某"
"些假设、保证、契约或不变性被打破的情况，比如当无效值、矛盾的值或缺失的值被传"
"递给代码时——此外还可能出现以下一种或多种情况："

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:85
msgid ""
"The bad state is something that is unexpected, as opposed to something that "
"will likely happen occasionally, like a user entering data in the wrong "
"format."
msgstr ""
"糟糕的状态是一种出乎意料的状况，而不是像用户以错误的格式输入数据这样的偶尔会"
"发生的情况。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:88
msgid ""
"Your code after this point needs to rely on not being in this bad state, "
"rather than checking for the problem at every step."
msgstr ""
"在此之后，你的代码需要依赖于不会进入这种糟糕的状态，而不是在每一步都检查是否"
"存在问题。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:90
msgid ""
"There’s not a good way to encode this information in the types you use. "
"We’ll work through an example of what we mean in [“Encoding States and "
"Behavior as Types”](ch18-03-oo-design-patterns.html#encoding-states-and-"
"behavior-as-types)<!-- ignore --> in Chapter 18."
msgstr ""
"你使用的类型并没有很好的方式来编码这些信息。我们将在第18章的[“将状态和行为编"
"码为类型”](ch18-03-oo-design-patterns.html#encoding-states-and-behavior-as-"
"types)<!-- ignore -->这一节中通过一个例子来说明我们的意思。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:94
msgid ""
"If someone calls your code and passes in values that don’t make sense, it’s "
"best to return an error if you can so that the user of the library can "
"decide what they want to do in that case. However, in cases where continuing "
"could be insecure or harmful, the best choice might be to call `panic!` and "
"alert the person using your library to the bug in their code so that they "
"can fix it during development. Similarly, `panic!` is often appropriate if "
"you’re calling external code that is out of your control and returns an "
"invalid state that you have no way of fixing."
msgstr ""
"如果有人调用你的代码并传入一些没有意义的值，最好能返回一个错误，这样库的使用"
"者就可以决定在这种情况下该怎么做。然而，在继续运行可能会带来安全隐患或损害的"
"情况下，最好的选择可能是调用 `panic!`，并提醒使用你库的人注意他们代码中的错"
"误，以便他们在开发过程中进行修复。同样，如果你调用的是外部代码，而这些代码不"
"受你的控制，并且返回了无法修复的无效状态，那么调用 `panic!` 通常是一个合适的"
"选择。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:103
msgid ""
"However, when failure is expected, it’s more appropriate to return a "
"`Result` than to make a `panic!` call. Examples include a parser being given "
"malformed data or an HTTP request returning a status that indicates you have "
"hit a rate limit. In these cases, returning a `Result` indicates that "
"failure is an expected possibility that the calling code must decide how to "
"handle."
msgstr ""
"然而，当故障是预期之中的时候，返回 `Result` 比调用 `panic!` 更为合适。例如，"
"当解析器接收到损坏的数据，或者HTTP请求返回的状态表明已经达到了速率限制时。在"
"这些情况下，返回 `Result` 意味着故障是一种预期中的可能性，调用代码必须自行决"
"定如何处理。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:109
msgid ""
"When your code performs an operation that could put a user at risk if it’s "
"called using invalid values, your code should verify the values are valid "
"first and panic if the values aren’t valid. This is mostly for safety "
"reasons: Attempting to operate on invalid data can expose your code to "
"vulnerabilities. This is the main reason the standard library will call "
"`panic!` if you attempt an out-of-bounds memory access: Trying to access "
"memory that doesn’t belong to the current data structure is a common "
"security problem. Functions often have _contracts_: Their behavior is only "
"guaranteed if the inputs meet particular requirements. Panicking when the "
"contract is violated makes sense because a contract violation always "
"indicates a caller-side bug, and it’s not a kind of error you want the "
"calling code to have to explicitly handle. In fact, there’s no reasonable "
"way for calling code to recover; the calling _programmers_ need to fix the "
"code. Contracts for a function, especially when a violation will cause a "
"panic, should be explained in the API documentation for the function."
msgstr ""
"当您的代码执行某个操作，而使用无效的值调用该操作可能会让用户面临风险时，您应"
"该先验证这些值是否有效，如果无效则触发panic。这主要是出于安全考虑：尝试处理无"
"效的数据可能会使代码暴露于漏洞风险之中。这也是为什么标准库会在您尝试进行越界"
"内存访问时调用 `panic!`：试图访问不属于当前数据结构的内存是一种常见的安全问"
"题。函数通常都有契约：只有当输入满足特定要求时，函数的行为才是可靠的。当违反"
"契约时触发panic是合理的，因为契约违反总是表明调用方存在错误，而这不是调用代码"
"需要显式处理的错误类型。实际上，调用代码没有合理的办法来恢复这种状况；需要由"
"调用方的技术人员来修复代码。对于函数的契约，尤其是当违反契约会导致panic时，应"
"该在该函数的API文档中进行说明。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:124
msgid ""
"However, having lots of error checks in all of your functions would be "
"verbose and annoying. Fortunately, you can use Rust’s type system (and thus "
"the type checking done by the compiler) to do many of the checks for you. If "
"your function has a particular type as a parameter, you can proceed with "
"your code’s logic knowing that the compiler has already ensured that you "
"have a valid value. For example, if you have a type rather than an `Option`, "
"your program expects to have _something_ rather than _nothing_. Your code "
"then doesn’t have to handle two cases for the `Some` and `None` variants: It "
"will only have one case for definitely having a value. Code trying to pass "
"nothing to your function won’t even compile, so your function doesn’t have "
"to check for that case at runtime. Another example is using an unsigned "
"integer type such as `u32`, which ensures that the parameter is never "
"negative."
msgstr ""
"不过，如果在所有函数中都进行大量的错误检查，不仅会使得代码变得冗长且令人烦"
"恼。幸运的是，你可以利用 Rust 的类型系统（以及编译器所进行的类型检查），来自"
"动完成许多检查工作。如果你的函数有一个特定的类型作为参数，那么你可以按照自己"
"的逻辑编写代码，而无需担心编译器已经确保了输入的值是有效的。例如，如果你使用"
"的是类型而不是 `Option`，那么程序期望得到的是某种值，而不是空值。这样，你的代"
"码就不需要处理 `Some` 和 `None` 这两种情况：它只需要处理一种存在有效值的情况"
"即可。试图向函数传递空值的代码根本无法编译，因此函数在运行时无需检查这种情"
"况。另一个例子是使用像 `u32` 这样的无符号整数类型，这种类型可以确保参数永远不"
"会是负值。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:139
msgid "<a id=\"creating-custom-types-for-validation\"></a>"
msgstr "<a id=\"creating-custom-types-for-validation\"></a>"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:141
msgid "Custom Types for Validation"
msgstr "用于验证的自定义类型"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:143
msgid ""
"Let’s take the idea of using Rust’s type system to ensure that we have a "
"valid value one step further and look at creating a custom type for "
"validation. Recall the guessing game in Chapter 2 in which our code asked "
"the user to guess a number between 1 and 100. We never validated that the "
"user’s guess was between those numbers before checking it against our secret "
"number; we only validated that the guess was positive. In this case, the "
"consequences were not very dire: Our output of “Too high” or “Too low” would "
"still be correct. But it would be a useful enhancement to guide the user "
"toward valid guesses and have different behavior when the user guesses a "
"number that’s out of range versus when the user types, for example, letters "
"instead."
msgstr ""
"让我们进一步利用 Rust 的类型系统来确保我们获得的是有效的数值，然后来看看如何"
"创建用于验证的自定义类型。回想一下第 2 章中的“猜数游戏”，我们的代码要求用户猜"
"测 1 到 100 之间的数字。在检查用户的猜测是否与我们的秘密数字匹配之前，我们从"
"未验证过用户的猜测是否在范围内；我们只是验证了猜测的数值是否为正数。在这种情"
"况下，后果并不严重：我们输出“太高”或“太低”仍然是正确的。但是，如果能引导用户"
"做出有效的猜测，并且在用户输入超出范围的数字时与输入字母时采取不同的处理方"
"式，那将是一个很好的改进。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:154
msgid ""
"One way to do this would be to parse the guess as an `i32` instead of only a "
"`u32` to allow potentially negative numbers, and then add a check for the "
"number being in range, like so:"
msgstr ""
"一种方法是将“guess”解析为 `i32`，而不仅仅是 `u32`，这样就能支持可能是负数的情"
"况。然后，需要添加一个检查，确保该数字在指定范围内，具体做法如下："

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:166
msgid "\"Guess the number!\""
msgstr "“猜测数字！”"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:171
#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:192
msgid "// --snip--\n"
msgstr ""
"// --snip--\n"
"——剪——\n"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:173
msgid "\"Please input your guess.\""
msgstr "“请输入你的猜测。”"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:179
msgid "\"Failed to read line\""
msgstr "“未猜对”"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:187
msgid "\"The secret number will be between 1 and 100.\""
msgstr "“机密号码将介于 1 到 100之间。”"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:193
msgid "\"Too small!\""
msgstr "“太小了！”"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:194
msgid "\"Too big!\""
msgstr "“太大了！”"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:196
msgid "\"You win!\""
msgstr "“你赢了！”"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:206
msgid ""
"The `if` expression checks whether our value is out of range, tells the user "
"about the problem, and calls `continue` to start the next iteration of the "
"loop and ask for another guess. After the `if` expression, we can proceed "
"with the comparisons between `guess` and the secret number knowing that "
"`guess` is between 1 and 100."
msgstr ""
"`if`表达式用于检查我们的数值是否超出了范围，并向用户提示存在的问题。之后会调"
"用`continue`来开始循环的下一次迭代，并再次请求用户输入一个猜测值。在`if`表达"
"式之后，我们可以继续比较`guess`与秘密数字之间的结果，同时知道`guess`的值应该"
"在1到100之间。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:212
msgid ""
"However, this is not an ideal solution: If it were absolutely critical that "
"the program only operated on values between 1 and 100, and it had many "
"functions with this requirement, having a check like this in every function "
"would be tedious (and might impact performance)."
msgstr ""
"然而，这并不是理想的解决方案：如果程序必须仅处理1到100之间的数值，并且有许多"
"函数都符合这一要求，那么在每个函数中都加入这样的检查将会非常繁琐（而且可能会"
"影响性能）。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:217
msgid ""
"Instead, we can make a new type in a dedicated module and put the "
"validations in a function to create an instance of the type rather than "
"repeating the validations everywhere. That way, it’s safe for functions to "
"use the new type in their signatures and confidently use the values they "
"receive. Listing 9-13 shows one way to define a `Guess` type that will only "
"create an instance of `Guess` if the `new` function receives a value between "
"1 and 100."
msgstr ""
"相反，我们可以在专门的模块中创建一个新的类型，并将验证功能放在一个函数中，用"
"于创建该类型的实例，而不是在各处重复这些验证。这样，函数可以在其签名中使用新"
"类型，并且可以放心地使用接收到的数值。列表9-13展示了一种定义 `Guess` 类型的方"
"法，该类型只有在 `new` 函数接收到的数值在1到100之间时，才会创建 `Guess` 类型"
"的实例。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:234
msgid "\"Guess value must be between 1 and 100, got {value}.\""
msgstr "\"猜测值必须介于1和100之间，得到{value}\"。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:248
msgid ""
"Note that this code in _src/guessing_game.rs_ depends on adding a module "
"declaration `mod guessing_game;` in _src/lib.rs_ that we haven’t shown here. "
"Within this new module’s file, we define a struct named `Guess` that has a "
"field named `value` that holds an `i32`. This is where the number will be "
"stored."
msgstr ""
"请注意，*src/guessing_game.rs* 中的代码依赖于在 *src/lib.rs* 中添加了一个模块"
"声明 `mod guessing_game;`，这一点我们在这里并未展示。在这个新模块的文件内，我"
"们定义了一个名为 `Guess` 的结构体，该结构体有一个名为 `value` 的字段，该字段"
"包含一个 `i32`。数字将被存储在这个字段中。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:254
msgid ""
"Then, we implement an associated function named `new` on `Guess` that "
"creates instances of `Guess` values. The `new` function is defined to have "
"one parameter named `value` of type `i32` and to return a `Guess`. The code "
"in the body of the `new` function tests `value` to make sure it’s between 1 "
"and 100. If `value` doesn’t pass this test, we make a `panic!` call, which "
"will alert the programmer who is writing the calling code that they have a "
"bug they need to fix, because creating a `Guess` with a `value` outside this "
"range would violate the contract that `Guess::new` is relying on. The "
"conditions in which `Guess::new` might panic should be discussed in its "
"public-facing API documentation; we’ll cover documentation conventions "
"indicating the possibility of a `panic!` in the API documentation that you "
"create in Chapter 14. If `value` does pass the test, we create a new `Guess` "
"with its `value` field set to the `value` parameter and return the `Guess`."
msgstr ""
"然后，我们在 `Guess` 上实现了一个名为 `new` `Guess` 的关联函数，该函数会创建 "
"`Guess` 的实例。 `new` 函数被定义为接受一个名为 `value` 的参数，该参数的类型"
"为 `i32`，并且返回一个 `Guess`。 `new` 函数的主体部分会测试 `value`，确保其值"
"在 1 到 100 之间。如果 `value` 未能通过此测试，我们将调用 `panic!`，这会提醒"
"编写调用代码的程序员他们有一个需要修复的错误，因为在此范围之外使用 `value` 创"
"建 `Guess` 会违反 `Guess::new` 所依赖的契约。 `Guess::new` 可能触发恐慌的条件"
"应在其公开 API 文档中讨论；我们将在第 14 章中介绍文档编写规范，说明 API 文档"
"中可能存在 `panic!` 的情况。如果 `value` 通过了测试，我们将创建一个新的 "
"`Guess`，并将其 `value` 字段设置为 `value` 参数，然后返回 `Guess`。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:268
msgid ""
"Next, we implement a method named `value` that borrows `self`, doesn’t have "
"any other parameters, and returns an `i32`. This kind of method is sometimes "
"called a _getter_ because its purpose is to get some data from its fields "
"and return it. This public method is necessary because the `value` field of "
"the `Guess` struct is private. It’s important that the `value` field be "
"private so that code using the `Guess` struct is not allowed to set `value` "
"directly: Code outside the `guessing_game` module _must_ use the `Guess::"
"new` function to create an instance of `Guess`, thereby ensuring that "
"there’s no way for a `Guess` to have a `value` that hasn’t been checked by "
"the conditions in the `Guess::new` function."
msgstr ""
"接下来，我们实现了一个名为 `value` 的方法。该方法继承了 `self` 的功能，没有任"
"何其他参数，并且返回一个 `i32`。这种方法有时被称为 _getter_，因为它的作用是从"
"其字段中获取一些数据并返回。这个公共方法是必要的，因为 `Guess` 结构体中的 "
"`value` 字段是私有的。确保 `value` 字段是私有的非常重要，这样使用 `Guess` 结"
"构体的代码就无法直接设置 `value`。 `guessing_game` 模块之外的代码必须使用 "
"`Guess::new` 函数来创建 `Guess` 的实例，从而确保没有 `Guess` 能够拥有未经 "
"`Guess::new` 函数中条件检查的 `value`。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:279
msgid ""
"A function that has a parameter or returns only numbers between 1 and 100 "
"could then declare in its signature that it takes or returns a `Guess` "
"rather than an `i32` and wouldn’t need to do any additional checks in its "
"body."
msgstr ""
"一个具有参数或仅返回1到100之间数字的函数，可以在其签名中声明它接受或返回一个 "
"`Guess` 类型的参数或返回值，而不需要在其主体中进行任何额外的检查。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:283
msgid "Summary"
msgstr "摘要"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:285
msgid ""
"Rust’s error-handling features are designed to help you write more robust "
"code. The `panic!` macro signals that your program is in a state it can’t "
"handle and lets you tell the process to stop instead of trying to proceed "
"with invalid or incorrect values. The `Result` enum uses Rust’s type system "
"to indicate that operations might fail in a way that your code could recover "
"from. You can use `Result` to tell code that calls your code that it needs "
"to handle potential success or failure as well. Using `panic!` and `Result` "
"in the appropriate situations will make your code more reliable in the face "
"of inevitable problems."
msgstr ""
"Rust的错误处理功能旨在帮助你编写更健壮的代码。`panic!`宏表示你的程序处于无法"
"处理的状态，它允许你让程序停止运行，而不是继续处理无效或错误的数值。`Result`"
"枚举利用Rust的类型系统来表明某些操作可能会失败，但你的代码可以从中恢复。你可"
"以使用`Result`来通知调用你的代码的代码，也需要处理可能出现的成功或失败情况。"
"在适当的场景下使用`panic!`和`Result`，可以使你的代码在面临不可避免的问题时更"
"加可靠。"

#: src/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.md:294
msgid ""
"Now that you’ve seen useful ways that the standard library uses generics "
"with the `Option` and `Result` enums, we’ll talk about how generics work and "
"how you can use them in your code."
msgstr ""
"既然你已经了解了标准库如何使用 `Option` 和 `Result` 枚举类型来实现泛型，接下"
"来我们将讨论泛型的工作原理，以及如何在你的代码中运用它们。"
